# AI Logo Repository Guide

How this repository is laid out and what adding an icon actually involves. [CONTRIBUTING.md](mdc:CONTRIBUTING.md) is the longer version and the one to trust if the two ever disagree.

## Project Overview

323 AI company, model and application brands, each a React component under `src/`, published as `@yldm-tech/ai-logo`. The repository is a pnpm workspace and pnpm is required — `vite` is aliased to `@voidzero-dev/vite-plus-core` through a catalog in `pnpm-workspace.yaml`, which no other package manager reads.

## Directory Structure

### Root Structure

- `src/` — one directory per brand, plus `features/` (the lookup components and their mapping tables), `hooks/`, `primitives/`, `types/`, `icons.ts` and the generated `toc.json`
- `apps/site/` — the website at <https://ailogo.yldm.ai>. It imports the package through `node_modules` the way a consumer does, so it is also the smoke test and carries the bundle budgets in `apps/site/treeshake`
- `packages/` — the unscoped `alias` package, the `react-native` port (private, not published), and `static-svg`, `static-png`, `static-webp`, `static-avatar`, which hold the committed renders and are published alongside the main package
- `scripts/` — `tocWorkflow` (generates `src/toc.json`), `readmeWorkflow` (the README icon table), `svgWorkflow` (the static renders), `rn-conversion` (the react-native port)
- `tests/` — contract tests over the mapping tables, the toc and the rendered assets

There is no `docs/` directory; the site under `apps/site` is the documentation.

### Icon Brand Structure

Each brand follows this pattern, and only `Mono.tsx` is required:

```
src/IconName/
├── index.ts          # attaches the subcomponents and the brand constants
├── style.ts          # TITLE, colours and the multiples
├── index.mdx         # frontmatter only — title, description, category
└── components/
    ├── Mono.tsx      # required, the default export
    ├── Color.tsx     # optional
    ├── Text.tsx      # optional wordmark
    ├── TextColor.tsx # optional
    ├── TextCn.tsx    # optional Chinese wordmark
    ├── Avatar.tsx    # optional
    └── Combine.tsx   # optional icon + wordmark
```

Which of these a brand has varies, and consumers read the attachments in `index.ts` as the contract: OpenAI is monochrome and has no `.Color`, so `<OpenAI.Color />` does not exist. Never write an example against a subcomponent without checking `src/<Brand>/index.ts` first.

## Adding a New Icon

Copy the closest existing brand rather than starting from scratch.

### 1. Style Configuration ([style.ts](mdc:src/AdobeFirefly/style.ts))

```typescript
export const TITLE = "YourIconName";
export const COMBINE_TEXT_MULTIPLE = 0.75; // used by Combine
export const COMBINE_SPACE_MULTIPLE = 0.3; // used by Combine
export const COLOR_PRIMARY = "#EB1000";

// Avatar constants
export const AVATAR_BACKGROUND = COLOR_PRIMARY;
export const AVATAR_COLOR = "#fff";
export const AVATAR_ICON_MULTIPLE = 0.6;
```

### 2. Monochrome Icon ([Mono.tsx](mdc:src/AdobeFirefly/components/Mono.tsx))

```typescript
"use client";

import { memo } from "react";

import type { IconType } from "@/types";

import { TITLE } from "../style";

const Icon: IconType = memo(({ size = "1em", style, ...rest }) => {
  return (
    <svg
      fill="currentColor"
      fillRule="evenodd"
      height={size}
      style={{ flex: "none", lineHeight: 1, ...style }}
      viewBox="0 0 24 24"
      width={size}
      xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
      {...rest}
    >
      <title>{TITLE}</title>
      <path d="YOUR_SVG_PATH_DATA" />
    </svg>
  );
});

export default Icon;
```

If the SVG needs `id` attributes, namespace them with `useFillIds` from [useFillId](mdc:src/hooks/useFillId.ts) rather than writing `id="a"`. Ids are global to the page: two icons that hardcode the same one clip each other, and so does any host page with its own.

### 3. Avatar Component ([Avatar.tsx](mdc:src/AdobeFirefly/components/Avatar.tsx))

```typescript
"use client";

import { memo } from "react";

import IconAvatar, { type IconAvatarProps } from "@/features/IconAvatar";

import { AVATAR_BACKGROUND, AVATAR_COLOR, AVATAR_ICON_MULTIPLE, TITLE } from "../style";
import Mono from "./Mono";

export type AvatarProps = Omit<IconAvatarProps, "Icon">;

const Avatar = memo<AvatarProps>(({ ...rest }) => {
  return (
    <IconAvatar
      Icon={Mono}
      aria-label={TITLE}
      background={AVATAR_BACKGROUND}
      color={AVATAR_COLOR}
      iconMultiple={AVATAR_ICON_MULTIPLE}
      {...rest}
    />
  );
});

export default Avatar;
```

### 4. Main Index File ([index.ts](mdc:src/AdobeFirefly/index.ts))

```typescript
import Avatar from "./components/Avatar";
import Color from "./components/Color";
import Combine from "./components/Combine";
import Mono from "./components/Mono";
import Text from "./components/Text";
import { COLOR_PRIMARY, TITLE } from "./style";

export type CompoundedIcon = typeof Mono & {
  Avatar: typeof Avatar;
  Color: typeof Color;
  Combine: typeof Combine;
  Text: typeof Text;
  colorPrimary: string;
  title: string;
};

const Icons = Mono as CompoundedIcon;
Icons.Color = Color;
Icons.Text = Text;
Icons.Combine = Combine;
Icons.Avatar = Avatar;
Icons.colorPrimary = COLOR_PRIMARY;
Icons.title = TITLE;

export default Icons;
```

The `Icons.X = X` lines are what `build:toc` reads to decide which variants the brand advertises. A component that exists in `components/` but is never attached is invisible to the toc, the README table, the site and the static renderer.

### 5. Documentation ([index.mdx](mdc:src/AdobeFirefly/index.mdx))

Frontmatter and nothing else. It is read by the toc script, which fails if the file is missing:

```mdx
---
title: Firefly (Adobe)
description: https://firefly.adobe.com
category: Application
---
```

`category` is one of `Model`, `Provider` or `Application`, lowercased into the `group` field the README columns and the site's filters are built from.

### 6. Export Integration

Add the brand to [src/icons.ts](mdc:src/icons.ts), in alphabetical position:

```typescript
export { default as YourIconName, type CompoundedIcon as YourIconNameProps } from "./YourIconName";
```

The toc script discovers icons by matching `default as (\w+)` in that file, so a brand missing from it does not exist downstream.

### 7. Lookup Mappings

An icon nobody can look up by id still renders the grey fallback. Add the brand to the mapping tables that apply: [providerEnum.ts](mdc:src/features/providerEnum.ts) and [providerConfig.tsx](mdc:src/features/providerConfig.tsx) for providers, [modelConfig.ts](mdc:src/features/modelConfig.ts) for models, [agentConfig.ts](mdc:src/features/agentConfig.ts) for applications. The react-native port duplicates the first three under `packages/react-native/src/features/`.

Provider keywords are compared with `===` after lowercasing, so the id must be listed verbatim. Model and agent keywords are unanchored regexes and the first match wins, so anchor them with `(^|/)` and `($|[-_])` unless the substring is really what you mean. [tests/contract.test.tsx](mdc:tests/contract.test.tsx) fails on a shadowed or orphaned keyword.

### 8. Generated Artifacts

```bash
pnpm run build:toc    # regenerates src/toc.json
pnpm run build:static # renders packages/static-* through headless Chrome — commit the new files
pnpm run sync:md      # regenerates the README icon table
npx remark --quiet --output -- README.md
```

All of these outputs are committed. CI regenerates the toc and the README and fails on a diff, and [tests/staticAssets.test.ts](mdc:tests/staticAssets.test.ts) fails if a variant the toc advertises has no rendered SVG behind it.

Porting the icon to react-native is `pnpm run auto-converter-web-icons-to-rn`, which converts what the port is missing and syncs its feature configs. Review that diff — it is code generation, not a build step.

## Development Workflow

| Task                  | Command               |
| --------------------- | --------------------- |
| Install               | `pnpm install`        |
| Dev server (the site) | `pnpm start`          |
| Build the package     | `pnpm run build`      |
| Test                  | `pnpm run test`       |
| Format, lint, types   | `pnpm run ci`         |
| Types only            | `pnpm run type-check` |

`pnpm run build` regenerates `src/toc.json` and then runs `vp pack`, which bundles `src/index.ts` into `es/index.mjs` with types alongside. `pnpm start` runs the site in `apps/site`.

## Usage

```tsx
import { Claude, ProviderIcon } from "@yldm-tech/ai-logo";

<Claude size={24} />
<Claude.Color size={24} />
<Claude.Combine size={24} type="color" />
<Claude.Avatar size={40} shape="circle" />
<ProviderIcon provider="anthropic" size={24} type="color" />;
```

`ProviderIcon`, `ModelIcon` and `AgentIcon` resolve an arbitrary id at runtime and therefore carry their whole mapping table — roughly 1.7 MB against 216 kB for a direct brand import. Use them when the id is dynamic, and the brand export when it is not.

## Important Notes

- `src/toc.json` is generated by `pnpm run build:toc`; `src/toc.ts` is the hand-written module that types and re-exports it. Neither is edited by hand.
- The static PNG, SVG, WebP and avatar files are generated by `pnpm run build:static` and committed. They are not produced in CI.
- Group types come from the `category` frontmatter: Model | Provider | Application.
- New icons use a `0 0 24 24` viewBox unless the artwork genuinely cannot fit one.
- Commits are gitmoji plus conventional commits — `:sparkles: feat: add the Mistral icon`. Without the emoji prefix the release analyser ignores the commit and nothing reaches npm.

## Best Practices

1. **Consistency**: follow the existing patterns and naming rather than inventing a new shape
2. **Accuracy**: check `index.ts` before claiming a brand has a variant
3. **Optimisation**: clean the SVG path and keep it to the 24x24 grid
4. **Testing**: `pnpm run ci` and `pnpm run test` before opening a pull request
5. **Accessibility**: keep the `<title>` in every icon and the `aria-label` on the wrappers
